WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.460
Cyrus Levinthal did the arithmetic in nineteen sixty-nine.

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.360
Even a small protein can adopt an astronomically large number of conformations.

3
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.520
Randomly sampling each one, even at the speed of bond rotation, would take far

4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.160
longer than the universe has existed.

5
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.480
The only escape from this paradox is that folding does not search randomly.

6
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.220
Instead, it follows biased pathways and stabilizes partly correct intermediates,

7
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.200
funneling rapidly downhill toward the native state.

8
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.540
Backbone geometry starts with the peptide bond itself.

9
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.420
Because that bond has partial double bond character from resonance, the six atoms

10
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.340
around it are locked flat, planarity.

11
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.620
That leaves only two backbone bonds free to rotate, phi and psi.

12
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.720
But even those are not unrestricted.

13
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:53.060
Steric clashes between neighboring atoms eliminate most combinations.

14
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.380
The Ramachandran plot maps favored backbone angles, which occupy only a small

15
00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.920
fraction of phi-psi space.

16
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.670
Water drives folding by pushing non-polar side chains together.

17
00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:09.730
Burying them in a hydrophobic core releases ordered water and is entropically

18
00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:14.510
favorable. Polar and charged residues sit on the surface of a soluble protein.

19
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.630
Membrane porins run the same logic in reverse.

20
00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.810
Hydrophobic residues face the lipid bilayer, and a polar channel runs through the

21
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.670
middle. Folding is at bottom a negotiation between the protein and the chemistry of

22
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:28.670
water.

23
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:33.163
Local constraints narrow the shapes a protein can explore, but do not solve the

24
00:01:33.163 --> 00:01:34.223
folding problem alone.

25
00:01:34.943 --> 00:01:37.323
Peptide bond planarity restricts backbone motion.

26
00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:39.963
The preference for trans bonds cuts further.

27
00:01:40.703 --> 00:01:44.223
Ramachandran allowed phi-psi combinations constrain the backbone angles.

28
00:01:44.943 --> 00:01:49.283
Finally, hydrophobic burial and other favorable contacts bias the search toward

29
00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:50.283
native structures.

30
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:52.383
Constraints are not limitations.

31
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:57.383
Together with favorable interactions, they help many sequences find a stable fold.

32
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:04.203
Alpha-1 antitrypsin shows how a single amino acid change, glutamate three four two

33
00:02:04.203 --> 00:02:08.283
to lysine, causes misfolding with consequences in two separate organs.

34
00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:13.003
In the liver, the misfolded protein aggregates inside cells instead of being

35
00:02:13.003 --> 00:02:14.603
secreted, damaging the liver.

36
00:02:15.303 --> 00:02:19.483
In the lungs, too little circulating protein leaves elastase poorly controlled,

37
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:22.403
allowing lung tissue destruction and emphysema.

38
00:02:22.983 --> 00:02:28.003
One mutation, two organ systems: a vivid demonstration of why folding fidelity

39
00:02:28.003 --> 00:02:28.463
matters.
